Candidiasis (Skin/Mucosal)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Beefy-red erythema, Satellite papules/pustules, Intertriginous distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-011
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe oropharyngeal and esophageal pain causing sudden inability to tolerate oral intake or swallow saliva.
Location: Nursing Home
Present at Visit: Patient, Registered Nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly, recently immunosuppressed patient experiencing acute, severe odynophagia and dysphagia resulting in complete cessation of oral fluid intake for the past 12 hours. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate the cause of the sudden dysphagia, rule out an obstructing foreign body or acute dehydration, and initiate targeted antifungal therapy to rapidly reverse the mucosal inflammation before profound dehydration or aspiration occurred.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My mouth and throat are on fire, and I can't even swallow my own spit. It hurts too much to drink."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 3-day history of worsening soreness in the mouth, progressing rapidly over the last 24 hours to severe odynophagia. They describe an intense burning sensation on the tongue and buccal mucosa. Over the last 12 hours, the patient has developed thick white curd-like plaques on the oropharyngeal mucosa and has been unable to swallow food, fluids, or their routine oral medications. The patient recently completed a 14-day course of broad-spectrum antibiotics for a urinary tract infection. The severe pain and inability to maintain hydration prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Nursing home resident, currently bed-bound due to weakness and dehydration.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk for acute kidney injury and delirium secondary to dehydration; inability to take essential daily medications.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Frail, acutely distressed, holding an emesis basin to spit out saliva, visibly dry mucous membranes.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals widespread, thick white curd-like plaques covering the buccal mucosa, hard palate, and posterior pharynx. Attempted scraping of the plaques utilizing a tongue depressor reveals easily bleeding mucosal surfaces that are intensely erythematous underneath. The patient demonstrates an impaired swallow with significant pain response. Abdominal exam is benign. No cutaneous intertriginous involvement.
Environmental Observations: A full pitcher of water remains untouched on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient recently finished Cephalexin. Routine antihypertensives currently withheld due to inability to swallow.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Oropharyngeal Candidiasis (Thrush) with presumed esophageal extension - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Viral Pharyngitis / Herpes Simplex - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of thick, curd-like white plaques that leave a raw bleeding base when scraped, which is classic for Candida, lacking grouped vesicles or punched-out ulcers.

Streptococcal Pharyngitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The widespread involvement of the buccal mucosa and palate, rather than isolated tonsillar exudates, and the absence of high fever or tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy.

Leukoplakia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute onset, painful nature, and fact that the plaques can be physically scraped off (leukoplakia is chronic, painless, and adherent).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, acute oropharyngeal candidiasis exacerbated by recent broad-spectrum antibiotic use, leading to critical odynophagia and a sudden halt in oral intake. Triage accurately flagged the rapid onset of dysphagia and impending dehydration in a frail elderly patient as a high-acuity scenario. Immediate bedside assessment was vital to differentiate this from a mechanical obstruction or severe viral infection, to supervise a trial of fluid intake, and to prescribe systemic oral antifungal drops/suspension to rapidly reduce the fungal load and mucosal inflammation. Delaying intervention until the morning would have inevitably led to profound dehydration, acute kidney injury, and the need for hospital admission for intravenous fluids.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Nystatin oral drops 100,000 units/mL (1mL QID) to be swished and swallowed. Administered a stat dose of paracetamol liquid for analgesia. Commenced a trial of small, frequent sips of cool fluids under nursing observation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By slowly sipping small amounts of ice water every 15 minutes instead of trying to drink a full glass at once, you will numb the back of your throat, making it easier to swallow and keeping your body hydrated. By holding the medicated drops in your mouth for as long as possible before swallowing, you will directly coat the yeast, killing it faster and rapidly stopping the severe burning.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Instructed facility staff on strict fluid balance monitoring.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: GP review in 24 hours to ensure fluid intake has resumed and odynophagia is resolving.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient remains completely unable to swallow any fluids for another 6 hours, becomes acutely confused, stops passing urine, or develops a high fever, nursing staff must arrange immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for IV hydration.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The fungal infection is severe and likely extends down the swallowing tube.
Clinical Course: The burning pain and difficulty swallowing should begin to improve within 24-48 hours of starting the antifungal drops. Full clearance will take 7-14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Nursing staff must monitor your fluid intake strictly. Inform them immediately if you feel you cannot swallow your saliva at all.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The recent antibiotics destroyed the normal healthy bacteria in your mouth, allowing the yeast to overgrow and cause severe inflammation. The drops will directly kill the yeast.
Actionable Steps: Swish the medication around your entire mouth for at least a minute before swallowing. Focus on continuous, small sips of cool fluids.

